The step size was chosen on the training seasons only: $K = 0.1$ logits (about 17 FIDE points) predicted the 2013-14 to 2016-17 games best. On 2017-18, Elo's mean log likelihood is −0.622 per game, against −0.658 for Bradley-Terry on 2016-17 alone, −0.681 for the constant and −0.697 for the pooled fit. Elo's edge over the pooled fit is 0.075 per game (standard error 0.010). The last line says why: the pooled strengths correlate only 0.33 with the strengths 2017-18 alone produces. [Glickman & Jones (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-statistics-040722-061813) analyse NBA outcomes with dynamic models in more depth.

Where does that leave the choice? When the data span a period over which agents plausibly change, a rating that forgets, such as Elo with $K$ chosen by prediction, may be sufficient for some purposes where a pooled fit is not. Over a short, fixed window, such as one tournament or one survey, the Bradley-Terry fit, with its standard errors and explicit terms for home advantage or order, may be the more useful summary. The first question to ask of the data is whether strength stayed put.

